## Reached at
  A Node-RED instance listens on TCP port 1880 and serves both its admin and
  runtime express applications on that port. The callers reach the
  implementation with plain HTTP GETs against it:

      requests.get(f"{BASE_URL}{endpoint}{name}", timeout=TIMEOUT)
      # BASE_URL = "http://localhost:1880", TIMEOUT = 10

  The implementation is the Node-RED dashboard plugin package
  `node-red-dashboard`, version 2.26.1, installed by the image build at:

      /data/node_modules/node-red-dashboard

  under Node-RED'"'"'s user directory `/data`. Node-RED itself (`node-red@1.2.7`,
  installed globally, binary `/usr/local/bin/node-red`) is started by the
  container entrypoint as:

      node-red --userDir /data

  and at startup it scans `node_modules` under the user directory for packages
  whose package.json declares a `node-red.nodes` map. This package declares:

      "node-red": { "nodes": { "ui_base": "nodes/ui_base.js", ... } }

  Node-RED requires `/data/node_modules/node-red-dashboard/nodes/ui_base.js`
  with the RED API, and at module load that file registers GET routes on
  `RED.httpAdmin`, the express application answering on port 1880 — among them
  `/ui_base/js/*`, `/ui_base/css/*`, `/ui_base/gs/*` and `/uisettings`. In the
  same load the module pulls in the package'"'"'s `ui.js`, which mounts the
  dashboard static UI at the path `ui` on `RED.httpNode` (same port) from the
  package'"'"'s `dist` directory. No deployed flow is needed: the routes exist as
  soon as Node-RED has loaded the package.

## Must provide
  The package directory `/data/node_modules/node-red-dashboard`, discoverable
  by Node-RED under the user directory:
    package.json             declares the `node-red.nodes` map, including
                             `"ui_base": "nodes/ui_base.js"`
    nodes/ui_base.js         a Node-RED node module,
                             `module.exports = function(RED) {...}`, which at
                             module load registers on `RED.httpAdmin`:
                               GET /ui_base/js/*
                               GET /ui_base/css/*
                               GET /ui_base/gs/*
                               GET /uisettings
                             and registers the node type with
                             `RED.nodes.registerType("ui_base", ...)`
    ui mount                 the dashboard static UI mounted at `/ui`
                             (`RED.httpNode` root plus `ui`), serving the
                             package'"'"'s `dist` directory
  Asset directories the routes resolve files against — project-owned, shipped
  with the package and its dependencies, and they must stay in place with
  their non-empty files:
    /data/node_modules/node-red-dashboard/dist/js
    /data/node_modules/node-red-dashboard/dist/css
    /data/node_modules/gridstack/dist
                             the directory of the gridstack package'"'"'s main
                             file (`dist/gridstack.js`); gridstack@0.6.4 is
                             installed flat alongside the dashboard package

## Called as
  GET /ui_base/js/*      req.params[0] — the Express wildcard capture: the
                         remainder of the URL path after the route prefix, a
                         single string
  GET /ui_base/css/*     same shape: one string in the wildcard capture
  GET /ui_base/gs/*      same shape: one string in the wildcard capture
  GET /uisettings        no inputs
  GET /ui                no inputs
  All inputs arrive in the URL path: no request bodies, no custom headers.
  Requests are plain HTTP GETs with a 10-second client timeout.

## Setup required
  - Node-RED must be running and answering on port 1880; the container
    entrypoint runs `node-red --userDir /data` in a restart loop, so the
    process restarts automatically after it is killed and edited package files
    take effect after a restart. The callers poll `GET /` for up to 60 seconds
    as a readiness gate before they exercise anything else, and `/` must keep
    answering.
  - The package must sit under the Node-RED user directory'"'"'s `node_modules`
    (`/data/node_modules`); that is the location Node-RED loads node packages
