## Reached at
  A PHP page of a Dolibarr 7.x application served by Apache in the application
  container (PHP 7.4, DocumentRoot /var/www/html/htdocs). The base URL is the
  APP_URL environment variable (default http://localhost). The handler callers
  reach is the product card page, staged at its runtime path by the image build
  inside the Dolibarr tree:

      /var/www/html/htdocs/product/card.php
      URL: <APP_URL>/product/card.php

  Every request goes through the application'"'"'s front controller (main.inc.php),
  pre-existing code of the same tree: it performs session authentication,
  exposes GETPOST() parameter access, runs the CSRF token machinery, and hands
  the handler its database handle. The handler'"'"'s create and update paths run
  through the application'"'"'s Product object layer (also pre-existing code in the
  tree), which is the component that touches the application'"'"'s MySQL database.
  Stored product state is observed through the application itself: the edit
  form HTML it renders, and the database configured in
  /var/www/html/htdocs/conf/conf.php (table prefix llx_, host db, database
  dolibarr). Callers address everything by base URL or absolute path; no
  working directory is involved.

## Must provide
  GET  /product/card.php?action=create&type=0
       product creation form page; embeds a hidden `token` input and the form
       fields that post back to the add entrypoint
  GET  /product/card.php?id=<product>&action=edit
       product edit form page; embeds a hidden `token` input and the product'"'"'s
       fields as named <input>/<select> controls (ref, label, statut,
       statut_buy, status_batch, finished, customcode, country_id,
       accountancy_code_sell, accountancy_code_buy)
  POST /product/card.php with action=add
       product creation entrypoint; consumes the create-form fields
  POST /product/card.php with action=update
       product update entrypoint; consumes the edit-form fields for the product
       named by id

## Called as
  All requests are form-encoded HTTP made with an authenticated session that
  sends a same-host Referer header on every request.

  GET <APP_URL>/product/card.php?action=create&type=0
  GET <APP_URL>/product/card.php?id=<product>&action=edit

  POST <APP_URL>/product/card.php   (creation)
      action                 form field; the string "add"
      type                   form field; a string
      token                  form field; a string scraped from the hidden token
                             input of the create form page fetched immediately
                             before
      ref                    form field; a string
      label                  form field; a string
      price                  form field; a string
      price_base_type        form field; a string
      statut                 form field; a string
      statut_buy             form field; a string
      status_batch           form field; a string
      finished               form field; a string
      customcode             form field; a string
      country_id             form field; a string
      units                  form field; a string
      accountancy_code_sell  form field; a string
      accountancy_code_buy   form field; a string

  POST <APP_URL>/product/card.php   (update)
      action                 form field; the string "update"
      id                     form field; a string
      token                  form field; a string scraped from the hidden token
                             input of that product'"'"'s edit form page, fetched
                             immediately before
      ref                    form field; a string
      label                  form field; a string
      statut                 form field; a string
      statut_buy             form field; a string
      status_batch           form field; a string
      finished               form field; a string
      customcode             form field; a string
      country_id             form field; a string
      units                  form field; a string
      accountancy_code_sell  form field; a string
      accountancy_code_buy   form field; a string

## Setup required
  - The application container is up: the image entrypoint has written
    /var/www/html/htdocs/conf/conf.php, loaded the schema and initial data into
    MySQL (host db, database dolibarr, table prefix llx_), created the admin
    account, and enabled the product module before Apache starts serving.
  - The caller logs in first (a form POST to <APP_URL>/index.php with the
    APP_USER / APP_PASS credentials, defaults admin / admin) and keeps the
    resulting session; the product card handler sits behind session
    authentication and per-user product permissions, which this session holds.
  - Each POST carries a fresh `token` form field taken from the hidden input of
    the form page fetched immediately before it, and the caller'"'"'s session sends
    a same-host Referer header because the application'"'"'s CSRF guard requires it
    on POST requests.
  - Stored-state read-back runs inside the same container: /usr/local/bin/php
    executes a caller-side probe script that parses
    /var/www/html/htdocs/conf/conf.php for the credentials and the table prefix
    and prints SELECT rows TAB-separated, so it always reads the very database
    the application uses.

## Test harness boundary
  - A pytest runner drives the application purely over HTTP through a shared
    helper module: it performs the login, fetches the form pages, scrapes the
    token and the <input>/<select> values out of the returned HTML, and issues
    the form-encoded POSTs above. None of that client-side code belongs to the
    implementation.
  - The update target is itself created through the action=add entrypoint
    during session setup, so the creation entrypoint is exercised by the
    calling side before any update flow runs.
  - The direct database read-back (the PHP probe described above) belongs to
    the caller; the implementation'"'"'s only involvement is that the database it
    reaches is the application'"'"'s own.
