      $url = $_SERVER['"'"'REQUEST_URI'"'"'];
      ...
      if (preg_match('"'"'/\/install_composer$/'"'"', $url) && $_SERVER['"'"'REQUEST_METHOD'"'"'] === '"'"'POST'"'"') {
          ...
          return;
      } elseif (!preg_match('"'"'/\/$/'"'"', $url)) {
          return;
      }
  A `GET /` request falls through to the inline HTML installer page that the
  same file emits after the PHP block. A `POST /install_composer` request is
  handled by the matched branch above.

## Must provide
  GET /                                     route serving the installer page (the inline HTML)
  POST /install_composer                    route, request URI ending in `/install_composer`, method POST
  $_POST['"'"'php_path'"'"']                        form field consumed by the POST /install_composer handler
  JSON response envelope                    body of the POST /install_composer response, a JSON object
  '"'"'result'"'"' key                              field present in the POST /install_composer JSON envelope
  command($phpPath)                         function in /app/composer_installer.php invoked by the POST handler with the posted php_path
  exec() of a built command line            the command() body runs PHP'"'"'s exec() against shell command lines built from the supplied php_path
  whichPhp()                                 function in /app/composer_installer.php (resolves a default php path for the form'"'"'s default value)

## Called as
  GET {BASE_URL}/
      No body. Serves the installer page (HTML).

  POST {BASE_URL}/install_composer
      Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
      data: {"php_path": <value>}
      php_path — single form field; a string.

  The POST handler then calls, in /app/composer_installer.php:
      command($_POST['"'"'php_path'"'"'])
      php_path — 1st positional argument to command(); a string.

  Inside command($phpPath) the supplied value is used to build the shell
  command line strings that are passed to exec() (the first uses the path to
  run a downloaded `composer.phar` installer; subsequent lines use it to run
  `composer.phar self-update` and `composer.phar update`), then the handler
  emits its JSON envelope.

## Setup required
  The PHP built-in server must be running on port 80 with document root
  `/app/webroot`. The image runs it through `/entrypoint.sh` in a restart loop,
  and the caller treats the service as ready once `GET /` answers.

  `/app/vendor/autoload.php` must be absent so the front controller dispatches
  to `/app/composer_installer.php`. The image build guarantees this by creating
  an empty `/app/vendor` and running no composer install.

  Writable (chmod 777 in the build) directories/files that command() checks
  before building command lines: `/app/composer`, `/app/vendor`, `/app/config`,
  `/app/tmp`, `/app/logs`, and `/app/composer.lock` (chmod 666); a missing
  writable target causes command() to throw before any exec().

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side runs a pytest suite that installs `pytest` and `requests`
  via `uv`, unsets proxy environment variables, defaults `BASE_URL` to
  `http://127.0.0.1`, waits for `GET /` to answer, and then issues HTTP
  requests to the service using the real `requests` library (the suite guards
  that `requests` resolves from `site-packages`, not a shadow module on the
  import path).

  The calling side also creates its own `/tmp` shell scripts as the `php_path`
  value and reads `/tmp` record/artifact files it authorises — those stub
  executables and log files belong to the caller, not to the implementation.
  The PHP built-in server, the entrypoint restart loop, and the `uv`/pytest
  runner are likewise part of the caller; the implementation is the PHP
  service reached over HTTP at `/` and `/install_composer`.
