Masked section 1:
```
- **Required interface:** `PJ_DEF(pj_status_t) pjmedia_rtcp_fb_build_nack(pjmedia_rtcp_session *session, void *buf, pj_size_t *length, unsigned nack_cnt, const pjmedia_rtcp_fb_nack nack[])` — exported from `libpjmedia`, declared in retained `pjmedia/rtcp_fb.h`; reached by direct harness callers and by `pjmedia/src/pjmedia/stream.c`/`vid_stream.c`. Uses file-local defines `RTCP_RTPFB` (205) and `RTCP_PSFB` (206) retained above this region, and the retained type `pjmedia_rtcp_common` (packed 8-byte header) plus `pjmedia_rtcp_fb_nack{ pj_int32_t pid; pj_uint16_t blp; }`.
- **Observable behavior:** Builds a Generic NACK RTCP-FB packet into `buf`. The feedback common header is seeded from the session'"'"'s cached RTCP common-header template (carrying the session'"'"'s sender SSRC), then `pt` is set to `RTCP_RTPFB` (205), `count` to 1 (FMT=1), and `length` to the network-byte-order value of `len/4 - 1`. Each NACK FCI entry is written as two network-order 16-bit words — `pid` then `blp` — beginning immediately after the feedback common header, 4 bytes per entry.
- **Inputs:** `session` (initialized via `pjmedia_rtcp_init`, so the session'"'"'s cached RTCP common-header template is populated), `buf`, `*length` (buffer capacity, in/out), `nack_cnt`, `nack[]`.
- **Outputs and errors:** On success sets `*length = (3 + nack_cnt) * 4` and returns `PJ_SUCCESS`. Returns `PJ_EINVAL` when any of `session`, `buf`, `length`, `nack_cnt`, `nack` is null/zero. Returns `PJ_ETOOSMALL` when the computed `len` exceeds `*length`.
- **Side effects and interactions:** Writes the packet bytes into the caller'"'"'s `buf`; reads the session'"'"'s cached RTCP common-header template. Produces PT=205/FMT=1 with the session'"'"'s sender SSRC.
- **Required sequencing and state:** `pj_init()` before the call; `pjmedia_rtcp_init(&session, ...)` before any build call so the session'"'"'s cached common-header template holds the sender SSRC.
- **Region boundary:** Self-contained — the range spans a whole function definition; the replacement opens and closes its own braces and leaves no surrounding construct open.
```

