        where pj_str_t is { char *ptr; pj_ssize_t slen; }

  Base type aliases in effect: pj_status_t = int, pj_size_t = size_t,
  pj_int32_t = int, pj_uint32_t = unsigned int, pj_uint16_t = unsigned
  short, pj_uint8_t = unsigned char, pj_bool_t = int.

## Called as
  Parse entry points. Each takes a raw packet buffer; callers pass the byte
  length of that buffer as the length argument (here `sizeof` of the array).
  The FCI-output arrays and the rpsi output are caller-allocated and passed
  by address.

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_parse_pli(buf, length)
          buf     — 1st positional; const void *; here an array of
                    unsigned char
          length  — 2nd positional; pj_size_t (size_t); here sizeof the
                    buffer

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_parse_nack(buf, length, nack_cnt, nack)
          buf       — 1st positional; const void *; here an array of
                      unsigned char
          length    — 2nd positional; pj_size_t; here sizeof the buffer
          nack_cnt  — 3rd positional; unsigned *; in/out count
          nack      — 4th positional; pjmedia_rtcp_fb_nack[] (caller-
                      allocated array)

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_parse_sli(buf, length, sli_cnt, sli)
          buf       — 1st positional; const void *; here an array of
                      unsigned char
          length    — 2nd positional; pj_size_t; here sizeof the buffer
          sli_cnt   — 3rd positional; unsigned *; in/out count
          sli       — 4th positional; pjmedia_rtcp_fb_sli[] (caller-
                      allocated array)

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_parse_rpsi(buf, length, rpsi)
          buf     — 1st positional; const void *; here an array of
                    unsigned char
          length  — 2nd positional; pj_size_t; here sizeof the buffer
          rpsi    — 3rd positional; pjmedia_rtcp_fb_rpsi * (single caller-
                    allocated struct, passed by address)

  Build entry points. Each takes a session by address and a caller-allocated
  output buffer with an in/out length; the count/struct args carry the FCI
  payload to encode. The session is first initialized via
  `pjmedia_rtcp_init` (see Setup required).

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_build_pli(session, buf, length)
          session — 1st positional; pjmedia_rtcp_session *
          buf     — 2nd positional; void *; caller-allocated byte buffer
          length  — 3rd positional; pj_size_t *; in (buffer capacity) /
                    out (produced length)

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_build_nack(session, buf, length, nack_cnt, nack)
          session   — 1st positional; pjmedia_rtcp_session *
          buf       — 2nd positional; void *; caller-allocated buffer
          length    — 3rd positional; pj_size_t *; in/out
          nack_cnt  — 4th positional; unsigned; count of entries
          nack      — 5th positional; const pjmedia_rtcp_fb_nack[]

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_build_sli(session, buf, length, sli_cnt, sli)
          session   — 1st positional; pjmedia_rtcp_session *
          buf       — 2nd positional; void *; caller-allocated buffer
          length    — 3rd positional; pj_size_t *; in/out
          sli_cnt   — 4th positional; unsigned; count of entries
          sli       — 5th positional; const pjmedia_rtcp_fb_sli[]

      pjmedia_rtcp_fb_build_rpsi(session, buf, length, rpsi)
          session — 1st positional; pjmedia_rtcp_session *
          buf     — 2nd positional; void *; caller-allocated buffer
          length  — 3rd positional; pj_size_t *; in/out
          rpsi    — 4th positional; const pjmedia_rtcp_fb_rpsi *

  The build harnesses populate the FCI input struct fields before the call:
  for nack, `.pid` and `.blp`; for sli, `.first`, `.number`, `.pict_id`;
  for rpsi, `.pt`, `.rpsi_bit_len`, and the embedded `pj_str_t` (`.rpsi.ptr`
  set to a byte buffer, `.rpsi.slen` set to its length).

  Session setup call used by every build harness:

      pjmedia_rtcp_init(&sess, name, clock_rate, samples_per_frame, ssrc)
          sess              — 1st positional; pjmedia_rtcp_session *
          name              — 2nd positional; char *; here a string literal
          clock_rate        — 3rd positional; unsigned
          samples_per_frame — 4th positional; unsigned
          ssrc              — 5th positional; pj_uint32_t (unsigned int)

## Setup required
  - `/app` must hold the built PJSIP tree with the static archives present
    at the `/app/<subdir>/lib/` paths above; the harness resolves them by
    glob `lib<base>-*.a` and selects the shortest match (so the base
    `libpjmedia-<target>.a` archive is chosen over the longer
    `-audiodev` / `-codec` / `-videodev` archives it also matches).
  - `pj_init()` must be called before any RTCP entry point; the harness
    calls it first in `main` and treats failure as a terminal condition.
  - For the build entry points, `pjmedia_rtcp_init(&sess, ...)` must be
    called on a `pjmedia_rtcp_session` before the build call, and
    `pjmedia_rtcp_fini(&sess)` is called after the build calls.
  - `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` must include
    `/app/pjlib/lib:/app/pjmedia/lib:/app/pjlib-util/lib:/app/pjnath/lib`
    at run time.
  - `/usr/bin/gcc` and the system libraries (`-lm -lpthread -lasound
    -luuid -lssl -lcrypto`) must be available for the harness link.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner that drives the target by compiling a
  fresh C harness from inline source: it writes a `.c` file under `/tests/`,
  invokes `/usr/bin/gcc` with the include directories and absolute-path
  archive link line shown above to produce a binary at `/tests/<name>`,
  then runs that binary through `subprocess` and reads `KEY=value` lines
  from its stdout. The inline C source, the `gcc` compile/link invocation,
  the `/tests/` working files, and the `KEY=value` parsing all belong to
  the caller, not to the implementation; the engineer does not rebuild them.
  The static archives and headers under `/app` are the implementation'"'"'s own
  build output and are what the harness binds to.
