## Called as
  Construction — the callers build a banking engine with caller-side fakes for
  every dependency except the bridge view:
      banking.New(
          log,                   // *logging.Logger
          cfg,                   // banking.Config
          col,                   // fake satisfying banking.Collateral
          witness,               // fake satisfying banking.Witness
          tsvc,                  // fake, GetTimeNow() time.Time
          assets,                // fake satisfying banking.Assets
          notary,                // fake satisfying banking.Notary
          broker,                // fake satisfying broker.Interface
          top,                   // fake, IsValidator() bool
          epoch,                 // fake, NotifyOnEpoch(...)
          marketActivityTracker, // fake satisfying banking.MarketActivityTracker
          bridges.NewERC20LogicView(ethClient, confs),
          ethEventSource,        // fake, UpdateCollateralStartingBlock(uint64)
      )
  where ethClient is the caller-side fake backend (satisfies bridges.ETHClient)
  and confs is a caller-side value whose Check(uint64) method exists.
  Engine entry points, all on *banking.Engine:
      e.DepositERC20(ctx, d, id, blockNumber, logIndex, txHash)
          ctx         — context.Context
          d           — *types.ERC20Deposit
          id          — a string
          blockNumber — a uint64
          logIndex    — a uint64
          txHash      — a string
      e.EnableERC20(ctx, al, id, blockNumber, txIndex, txHash)
          al — *types.ERC20AssetList; remaining inputs as above
      e.UpdateERC20(ctx, event, id, blockNumber, txIndex, txHash)
          event — *types.ERC20AssetLimitsUpdated; remaining inputs as above
      e.BridgeStopped(ctx, stopped, id, block, logIndex, ethTxHash)
          stopped — a bool; id, ethTxHash strings; block, logIndex uint64
      e.BridgeResumed(ctx, resumed, id, block, logIndex, ethTxHash)
          resumed — a bool; remaining inputs as above
  Each entry point hands a validators.Resource to the injected witness'"'"'s
  StartCheck; the caller-side witness runs that resource'"'"'s Check()
  synchronously and the entry-point call is observed through it, so contact
  with the on-chain lookup happens inside that resource check.
  Withdrawal lookup, reached by reflection on the concrete view:
      view := bridges.NewERC20LogicView(ethClient, confs)
      m := reflect.ValueOf(view).MethodByName("FindWithdrawal")
      args := []reflect.Value{
          reflect.ValueOf(w),            // *types.ERC20Withdrawal
          reflect.ValueOf(blockNumber),  // uint64
          reflect.ValueOf(logIndex),     // uint64
          reflect.ValueOf(assetAddr),    // string
      }
  When m.Type().NumIn() is 5 the callers append one more argument, a string,
  before calling. The callers then index the returned tuple at position 1 as
  a string and at position 3 as an error. FindWithdrawal therefore has 4 or 5
  inputs (a trailing string input when it has 5) and a return tuple of at
  least 4 values whose slot 1 is a string and slot 3 is an error.
  Staking verifier:
      v := staking.NewOnChainVerifier(
          staking.NewDefaultConfig(), logging.NewTestLogger(), ethClient, confs)
      v.UpdateStakingBridgeAddresses(addrs)  // addrs — []ethcommon.Address,
                                             // one element
      v.CheckStakeDeposited(ev)   // ev — *types.StakeDeposited
      v.CheckStakeRemoved(ev)     // ev — *types.StakeRemoved
  Multisig verifier:
      v := erc20multisig.NewOnChainVerifier(
          erc20multisig.NewDefaultConfig(), logging.NewTestLogger(),
          ethClient, confs)
      v.UpdateMultiSigAddress(addr)        // addr — ethcommon.Address
      v.CheckThresholdSetEvent(ev)         // ev — *types.SignerThresholdSetEvent
