      /app/src/pyload/core/config/parser.py

  The implementation is the pyLoad download-manager package (`pyload-ng`, src
  layout), installed editable from `/app`, under CPython 3.9. `ConfigParser` is
  instantiated per caller over a scratch user directory and reached only through
  its methods; there is no CLI, HTTP route, or service in play.

## Must provide
  pyload.core.config.parser           importable module, file == /app/src/pyload/core/config/parser.py
  ConfigParser                        class, defined in that module file (its defining file is checked too)
  ConfigParser.__init__(userdir)      constructor taking one path argument
  ConfigParser.cast(typ, value)       instance method; dispatches on a type-name string; the "time" branch is the one callers exercise here, alongside "int", "float", "str", "bool"
  ConfigParser.parse_config(path)     instance method; reads and parses a config file path
  ConfigParser.get(section, option)   instance method
  ConfigParser.set(section, option, value)   instance method
  ConfigParser.__getitem__(section)   returns a section proxy supporting item assignment `proxy[option] = value`
  ConfigParser.add_plugin_config(name, config)   instance method; optional third `outline` argument exists but callers do not pass it
  ConfigParser.set_plugin(plugin, option, value)   instance method
  ConfigParser.get_plugin(plugin, option)   instance method

## Called as
  parser_mod.ConfigParser(userdir)
      userdir — 1st positional argument; a string directory path (callers pass a
      freshly created temp dir)

  parser.cast(typ, value)
      typ   — 1st positional argument; a string type name; callers drive
              "time", "int", "float", "str", "bool"
      value — 2nd positional argument; a string or None

  parser.parse_config(config_file)
      config_file — 1st positional argument; a string path to a config file whose
      lines are of the shape `<type> <name> : "<desc>" = <value>` grouped under
      section headers `<section> - "<desc>":` (callers also drive the "time" type
      through this method by including `time` option lines in the file)

  parser.set(section, option, value)
      section — 1st positional argument; a string section name
      option  — 2nd positional argument; a string option name
      value   — 3rd positional argument; a string

  parser.get(section, option)
      section — 1st positional argument; a string section name
      option  — 2nd positional argument; a string option name

  parser["download"]["end_time"] = value
      section proxy — `parser[section]` takes a string section name; the returned
      proxy receives `[option] = value` where option is a string and value is a
      string (this routes into the same write path as `set`)

  parser.add_plugin_config(name, config)
      name   — 1st positional argument; a string plugin name
      config — 2nd positional argument; a list of 4-tuples
               (option_name, type_name, desc, default); each element a string,
               with type_name "time" exercised here

  parser.set_plugin(plugin, option, value)
      plugin — 1st positional argument; a string plugin name
      option — 2nd positional argument; a string option name
      value  — 3rd positional argument; a string

  parser.get_plugin(plugin, option)
      plugin — 1st positional argument; a string plugin name
      option — 2nd positional argument; a string option name

## Setup required
  The package must be importable as `pyload` from `/app/src` (editable install of
  `pyload-ng`; CPython 3.9). `pyload/__init__.py` (pre-existing project code, not
  part of the rebuild) supplies `PKGDIR` and `exc_logger`, which the parser module
  imports.

  The constructor creates `<userdir>/settings/`, copies the package default config
  into it, and parses it. The package ships a default config resource at
  `pyload/core/config/default.cfg` (project-owned, read by the constructor) that
  pre-registers the `download` section with `start_time` and `end_time` options of
  type `time`; this is what makes `set("download", "start_time", ...)` and
  `parser["download"]["end_time"] = ...` resolve, and it is the same resource that
  first routes a `time` value through `cast` during construction. `userdir` must be
  writable so the constructor can create and write its settings files.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner loads the test modules; a fixture constructs `ConfigParser` over a
  throwaway `tempfile.mkdtemp` user directory and removes it afterward; a second
  fixture writes throwaway `.cfg` text files (via `tempfile.mkstemp`) to feed
  `parse_config` and removes them afterward. The runner also asserts module/class
  origin with `inspect.getfile`/`os.path.realpath`. All of this — the pytest runner,
  the temp dirs, the temp config files, and the origin assertions — belongs to the
  calling side, not to the implementation; the engineer does not rebuild it.
